\f1\fs22 Eloise Shakti\'92s downtown office sat precisely at the intersection of taste and intimidation. The paintings adorning the looked suspiciously like accidents involving industrial paint supplies, yet somehow had appraised values that could sustain a small nation for several fiscal quarters. Her desk was carved from a single piece of rainforest hardwood that Eloise acquired after seventeen environmental exemptions and the sudden, mysterious retirement of three government officials.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander slouched in the visitor\'91s chair across from his mother, actively cultivating an air of disinterest that took considerable effort to maintain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Darling,\'91 said Eloise, in the tone that successful corporate executives reserve for particularly tricky board meetings and children they\'91re about to disappoint expensively. \'91I have a tremendous opportunity for you.\'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'91s internal alarm system went to DEFCON 2. \'93Tremendous opportunity\'94 was Eloise-speak for \'93inconvenient reassignment disguised as a promotion.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m listening,\'92 he said, and mentally calculated the odds he could reach the door before his mother activated whatever hidden security measures she\'92d undoubtedly installed since their last disagreement. The odds weren\'92t good.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91As you might know,\'92 Eloise continued, aware Alexander knew nothing of any of her business. Most of what she was about to tell was fabrication. As one of several non-verbal queues she used to keep Alexander\'92s attention, she leaned forward slightly. \'91Shakti Industries has recently acquired VorpalTech AG in the charming principality of Liechtenstein.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Liechtenstein?\'92 Alexander blinked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, that adorable postage stamp of a country wedged between Switzerland and Austria\'92. The entire nation could fit inside the old Olympic district with room left over for a long-term car park. In this universe their primary exports were false teeth, postage stamps, and financial regulations with conveniently sized loopholes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And what exactly does VorpalTech do?\'92 Alexander asked, already dreading the answer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Officially?\'91 Eloise dismissively waved a manicured hand. \'91Advanced telecommunications infrastructure. Unofficially? We\'91re not entirely sure. Their financial reports contain seventeen different currencies, including three that don\'91t actually exist. Their R&D department has filed patents for devices that, according to our legal team, violate not only international law but several fundamental constants of physics. And their CEO communicates exclusively through interpretive dance and the occasional haiku.\'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander slouched further into his chair. \'91And you want me to\'85\'92 He made some rolling hand-waving of his own.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Take over as Supervising Executive Director of Pan-European Audiovisual Communications and Strategic Insights,\'92 Eloise announced with a carnivorous smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s not a real job title.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That wiped away Eloise\'92s customary I\'92m-in-charge smile. \'91It is now. I had it printed on business cards.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91When?\'92 Alexander scoffed. \'91This morning?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise made herself shrug then slid a small box across the desk. The cards inside were embossed with gold leaf and smelled faintly of expensive cologne and regret.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s pupils dilated, and Eloise could see her son was already hooked. He flipped one over in his hand, savouring it weight and texture, and its classic, understated smugness. \'91This is just a fancy way of saying you\'92re sending me to the corporate equivalent of Siberia,\'91 he covered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Liechtenstein is lovely this time of year,\'91 Eloise countered. The blizzards were weeks away yet. \'91And consider the timing. Sarah\'91s pregnancy has reached that\'85\'92\'97she searched for an appropriately diplomatic phrase\'97\'91aesthetically challenging phase. I imagine your camera finds it difficult to capture her best angles these days.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander shifted uncomfortably. He was thinking only the previous night at the reception, during the joint venture waltz in which the happy couple tried to waltz to a favourite song that was invariable in 4/4 time, that Sarah had transformed from a svelte, intriguing subject for his documenter male gaze into more of a sentient beach ball with hormonal mood swings that could give a gymnast whiplash. His artistic sensibilities were offended, though he\'91d never admit as much to his mother without full body armour and an escape hover-copt standing by.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s more,\'92 Eloise said, noting his hesitation. \'91VorpalTech has been developing something they call \'93Quantum Reality Lenses\'94. Their marketing materials\'97which admittedly are little better than crayon drawings and equations scribbled on napkins\'97suggest these devices can capture images across multiple dimensional planes simultaneously.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s professional interest flared despite himself. \'91Multiple dimensions? You mean like parallel universes?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise pursed her lips and grimaced more than she intended. \'91Imagine the documentary potential. 
\f2\i Candid Criminal: Into the Multiverse
\f1\i0 . You could film the road not taken. The alternative realities. Versions where your marks actually committed the crimes you accuse them of.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91All my 
\f2\i marks,
\f1\i0  as you call them, are guilty of something,\'92 Alexander muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Typical Tramp line: accuse them of everything, convict them of the easiest thing to make stick,\'92 Eloise scoffed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s mind was already racing with possibilities. If these lenses worked, it would be the scoop of the century. He could capture footage no other filmmaker had ever dreamed of.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise pressed her advantage. \'91There\'92s also the matter of certain\'85 political pressures. It would be prudent to  keep you at a safe distance for a while.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What political pressure?\'92 Alexander demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise waited for him to invent his own implausible plausibilities. What he said raised even her eyebrow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s Dunhilton isn\'92t it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise waited, neither confirming nor denying.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He\'92ll do anything to block me becoming CEO of this fair gameshow-loving nation.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise brushed a wisp of hair from her forehead, willing her eyebrow to lose its Eiffel Tower-shaped arch. \'91So you 
\f2\i do
\f1\i0  see \'85 with the kind of perception only you possess \'85 this assignment offers you professional advancement, artistic opportunity, while waiting out Dunhilton.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, well, I have been working on a story treatment. Give him enough space to try something, which will lead to his downfall,\'92 \'97Alexander fingered the business cards\'97his business cards\'97 \'91and my triumphant return.\'92 He selected a card, flicked it with a finger nail, and smiled at its sturdiness. \'91How long?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just until things settle down here. Until the Sarah situation resolves itself, one way or another. Soon I may have a little surprise for Dunhilton and his ilk.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Really?\'92 Alexander sat up straight, like he imagined a CEO might at a cabinet meeting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise nodded subtly, too subtle for Alexander to notice. \'91Six months at most. Besides,\'92 Eloise added, her voice dropping to a conspiratorial whisper, \'91there are rumours that VorpalTech has recently bought what the locals call a multidimensional dairy farm. Workers report strange phenomena\'97\cf0 Braunvieh cows that have two heads at night, cheese that simultaneously tastes like Roquefort and Monterey Jack, milk urns that never fill up, a staff \cf2 cafeteria serving edible food on Tuesdays\'85\'92 Eloise allowed herself theta weary expression that suggested she could go on, but she\'92d rather make money or put an adversary back in their place\'97both of which she frequently achieved with the one action.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And you want me to document this?\'92 Alexander\'92s eyebrows rose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I want you to investigate discreetly, document thoroughly, and most importantly, stay out of trouble until I send for you.\'92 Eloise\'92s smile had all the warmth of a tax auditor who\'92s just found a significant discrepancy. \'91The company jet is fuelled and waiting. Your bags have already been packed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander opened his mouth to protest this invasion of privacy, then closed it again. There was little point arguing with Eloise when she\'92d already set things in motion. Besides, the prospect of documenting dimensional anomalies with experimental quantum camera equipment was intriguing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fine,\'92 he said finally. \'91But I want full creative control, an unlimited expense account, and a guarantee that you\'92ll recall me when things get interesting here.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course, darling. I wouldn\'92t dream of missing your triumphant return when the time is right.\'92 Eloise pressed a button on her desk. \'91Now, do hurry. The pilot has strict instructions not to serve the good champagne until you\'92re at cruising altitude.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The best champagne, mother?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course. We both know how tetchy you get with beverages that cost less than a small automobile.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They give me gas.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course they do, darling. It\'92s an old trick the well-off reserve for the aspirational: feed them faux brands that are actually worse than the cheap stuff.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander nodded happily, always eager to book the best seats to a class war. \'91That way, they\'92ll congratulate themselves for buying even cheaper crap, believing it\'92s actually the best available.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They stood and embraced in a manner that might have taken a pair of legal armies months to negotiate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92That\'92s my boy,\'92 Eloise said with just enough warmth to mask the underlying chill-factor. Then, she added casually, \'91Oh, I should mention, three of VorpalTech\'92s directors resigned under mysterious circumstances. The first claimed he was being stalked by his own shadow, the second insisted his reflection was plotting against him, and the third... well, the third technically didn\'92t resign so much as fold himself into the eleventh dimension during a particularly heated board meeting.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander paused at the door. \'91You\'92re joking.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91About the eleventh dimension? Yes, of course. Current theoretical physics only supports ten at most. \'91Eloise smiled serenely. \'91Safe travels, darling. Do try not to accidentally film the end of the world as we know it while you\'92re away.\'92
